      "id": 1,
      "claim": "The belief that flies arise from rotting meat is the doctrine of spontaneous generation.",
      "evidence": "Historical biology texts describe spontaneous generation as the idea that life can originate from non‑living matter, e.g., flies from meat.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": []
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "claim": "Francesco Redi (1668) disproved spontaneous generation of flies by showing that maggots appear only when meat is exposed to adult flies.",
      "evidence": "Redi placed meat in three jars: open, covered with gauze, and sealed; only the open jar produced maggots, demonstrating they came from eggs laid by flies.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "claim": "Louis Pasteur (1859) later disproved spontaneous generation of microorganisms with swan‑neck flask experiments.",
      "evidence": "Broth in flasks remained sterile unless the neck was broken, showing that airborne particles, not the broth itself, caused growth.",
      "weight": 0.25,
      "depends_on": [1]
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "claim": "Both Redi’s and Pasteur’s conclusions relied on systematic, repeated experimental controls rather than on a single new instrument.",
      "evidence": "Redi’s experiment required no microscope; Pasteur’s relied on the design of the flask and observation of turbidity, not on microscopic observation of organisms.",
      "weight": 0.20,
      "depends_on": [2, 3]
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "claim": "Therefore the idea was disproved primarily because of continued experimentation (the scientific method).",
      "evidence": "The historical record attributes the refutation of spontaneous generation to the series of controlled experiments by Redi, Pasteur, and others.",
      "weight": 0.10,
      "depends_on": [4]
    }
  ],
  "answer": "C",
